README-KERMIT -- six kermits, and which one to takeIf you are copying part of this collection onto your own media you want ONEkermit.  This file is here so you do not have to install several and try them.Short answer: take `ckermit' if you can spare 348K and have Microware's `cio',and `kermit' if you cannot.  ckermit is the real Columbia C-Kermit with acommand language, scripting and help built in; `kermit' is the small 1980sOS-9 port that needs nothing but itself.THE SIX FILES-------------  file                bytes   needs  module        what it says it is                              cio  -----------------------------------------------------------------------  kermit              26828   no     kermit        OS-9 Kermit Version 1                                                   Release 5  kermit2             21998   no     kermit2       Kermit Program Version 1                                                   Release 6  kermit3             18148   no     Kermit        Kermit68K version 1.0.00,                                                   01 July 1987  xkermit             14176   yes    xkermit       same banner as kermit --                                                   Version 1 Release 5  ckermit            356040   yes    wermit        C-Kermit 5A(190) BETA.14,                                                   24 Jul 94  REBUILT/kermit_cio  13294   yes    kermit_cio    our build of the same                                                   source as kermitEvery banner above was read off the running program on 2026-08-31, not froman index entry.  DOC/INDEX had `kermit' recorded as C-Kermit 5A(188) andstarred until that day; it is neither.Documentation: DOC/kermit (2 files), DOC/kermit2, DOC/kermit3 and DOC/ckermit(1 each).  Source: SRC/kermit, which is the 1.5 line -- kermit, xkermit andREBUILT/kermit_cio all come from it.  ckermit, kermit2 and kermit3 arrived asbinaries.THEY NO LONGER FIGHT OVER ONE MODULE NAME-----------------------------------------Until 2026-08-31 `kermit', `kermit2' and `REBUILT/kermit' all registered themodule name `kermit'.  OS-9 finds a program by module name once it isresident, whatever path you type, so loading any one of them answered for theothers.  Each now answers to its own filename.  `kermit3' registers `Kermit'with a capital K -- that is the author's own name for it and it nevercollided.TWO FLAGS DECIDE WHETHER THE TRANSFER WORKS-------------------------------------------The four command-letter kermits -- kermit, kermit2, xkermit and kermit_cio --take their flags as ONE bundled argument with no dash:    kermit c[le line esc.char]      connect    kermit s[difl line] file ...    send    kermit r[difl line]             receive  - **`l' is what aims it at a device.**  `kermit s /t1 file' sends over your    own console and ignores `/t1' without saying so; `kermit sl /t1 file' uses    the device.  Neither reports an error.  The giveaway is transfer traffic    appearing on the terminal you are sitting at.  - **For binary, `i' (image), never `8'.**  `8' is 8-bit quoting, for a link    that is not 8-bit clean, and it corrupts data over a path that already is.    `kermit sil /t1 mymodule' moves an OS-9 module intact.ckermit does not work this way: it has its own command language(`Kermit-68K>' prompt, `set line', `send', `help'), which is most of why it isthirteen times the size.TAKE THIS ONE IF----------------  ckermit    You want the real thing: a command language, scripts, server             mode, and `help' that answers.  Costs 348K and Microware's cio.             It is a BETA build (5A(190) BETA.14) and says so on startup.  kermit     You want the smallest thing that transfers a file and needs             nothing from Microware.  The 1.5 port, with source in SRC/kermit             and two files of documentation.  This is the one to take if you             are short of either space or runtime modules.  kermit2    Release 6 against kermit's Release 5, and 5K smaller.  Same             command letters.  Nothing was found that it does and kermit does             not, so there is little reason to prefer it -- but if you want the             later of the two small ones, this is it.  kermit3    A different program despite the name: Kermit68K 1.0.00 from 1987,             which puts up its own `Kermit68K>' prompt and reads a `Kermit.ini'             at startup rather than taking command letters.  Take it if you             want a command prompt without ckermit's size.  xkermit    The same version and banner as `kermit' in half the space,             because it links cio instead of carrying stdio.  Take it only if             you already have cio and want the smaller file.  REBUILT/kermit_cio             Our own build of SRC/kermit.  It needs cio where the archive's             own `kermit' does not, so there is no reason to take it over             `kermit'.  It is here because the recipe is here.CHOOSING--------  You want scripting, a command language and help    ckermit  You have no cio and do not want to need one        kermit  (or kermit2)  You are short of disk space and have cio           xkermit  You want a prompt but not 348K                     kermit3  You want the source as well as the binary          kermit  If you take exactly one file and nothing else: `kermit'.  It is the only one  that is small, needs no Microware module, has its source here and has its  documentation here.